```c
void bubble_sort(int *arr, int n) {
    for (int end = n - 1; end > 0; end--) {
        int swapped = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < end; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                int t = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[i + 1];
                arr[i + 1] = t;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
}
```

Repeatedly sweep through the array, swapping neighbours that are out of order; each pass pushes the largest remaining value to the end. Stop early if a pass makes no swaps. C has no built-in array length or string type, so the length is passed explicitly or the string is NUL-terminated.

**Complexity:** O(n²) time, O(1) extra space; fine for learning, but use the standard library sort in real code.

For example, `bubble_sort([5, 2, 9, 1, 7])` gives `1 2 5 7 9`.

