```python
def merge_sort(items):
    if len(items) <= 1:
        return list(items)
    mid = len(items) // 2
    left, right = merge_sort(items[:mid]), merge_sort(items[mid:])
    merged, i, j = [], 0, 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] <= right[j]:
            merged.append(left[i]); i += 1
        else:
            merged.append(right[j]); j += 1
    return merged + left[i:] + right[j:]
```

Split in half, sort each half, then merge. It is O(n log n) in every case and stable, at the cost of O(n) extra space.

