Here's a C++ version:

```cpp
vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int> arr) {
    for (int end = (int)arr.size() - 1; end > 0; end--) {
        bool swapped = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < end; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                swap(arr[i], arr[i + 1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
    return arr;
}
```

Repeatedly sweep through the array, swapping neighbours that are out of order; each pass pushes the largest remaining value to the end. Stop early if a pass makes no swaps. Pass large containers by const reference (as here) to avoid copying them.

**Complexity:** O(n²) time, O(1) extra space; fine for learning, but use the standard library sort in real code.

For example, `bubble_sort([5, 2, 9, 1, 7])` gives `1 2 5 7 9`.

